{
  "case_id": "2019-18-107",
  "document_hash": "cf1a12f36de8976e980e1ca7c913d4bc71463d6a7a404e2c6a252a883a4a191e",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2019-18-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cf1a12f36de8976e980e1ca7c913d4bc71463d6a7a404e2c6a252a883a4a191e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cf1a12f36de8-0000-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:08 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent Aimee Stephens",
      "case_id": "2019-18-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cf1a12f36de8976e980e1ca7c913d4bc71463d6a7a404e2c6a252a883a4a191e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cf1a12f36de8-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. COLE: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: Aimee Stephens is a transgender woman. She was a valued employee of Harris Funeral Homes for six years, until she told her boss that she was going to live and identify as a woman. When Harris Homes responded by firing her, it discriminated against her because of her sex for three reasons:"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Cole",
      "case_id": "2019-18-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cf1a12f36de8976e980e1ca7c913d4bc71463d6a7a404e2c6a252a883a4a191e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cf1a12f36de8-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, I think, I mean, the Sixth Circuit expressly said that the reasons for firing her extended beyond the dress code. Counsel for Harris Homes conceded at oral argument in the Second Circuit that she would have been fired if she showed up as a woman, even if she were following the dress code."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE GORSUCH",
      "case_id": "2019-18-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cf1a12f36de8976e980e1ca7c913d4bc71463d6a7a404e2c6a252a883a4a191e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cf1a12f36de8-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So, Mr. Cole, though, your argument, though, doesn't turn on that. I mean, it -- as I understand it, again, that if -- if the firing had been solely what the employer claims, the basis of the dress code only, the result would be the same."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE GORSUCH",
      "case_id": "2019-18-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cf1a12f36de8976e980e1ca7c913d4bc71463d6a7a404e2c6a252a883a4a191e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cf1a12f36de8-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Judge Lynch is a very thoughtful judge and -- and wrote a very thoughtful opinion that I -- I think he probably regretted having to write. What do you say to -- to him?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Cole",
      "case_id": "2019-18-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cf1a12f36de8976e980e1ca7c913d4bc71463d6a7a404e2c6a252a883a4a191e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cf1a12f36de8-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I -- I say that recognizing that transgender people have a right to exist in the workplace and not be turned away because of who they are does not end dress codes or restrooms. There are transgender lawyers in this courtroom today."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. BURSCH",
      "case_id": "2019-18-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cf1a12f36de8976e980e1ca7c913d4bc71463d6a7a404e2c6a252a883a4a191e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cf1a12f36de8-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Treating women and men equally does not mean employers have to treat men as women. That is because sex and transgender status are independent concepts."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Francisco",
      "case_id": "2019-18-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cf1a12f36de8976e980e1ca7c913d4bc71463d6a7a404e2c6a252a883a4a191e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cf1a12f36de8-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "One other point on the restroom scenario. Gender identity is a broad concept. You could have a male employee who identifies as a woman but doesn't dress as a woman, looks like a man, showing up in the shower and the locker room, and, again, the employer wouldn't be able to do anything about that because under Mr. Cole's theory, but for the fact he was a man, he could be there. And it's stereotyping to say men cannot be in the women's bathroom."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Francisco",
      "case_id": "2019-18-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cf1a12f36de8976e980e1ca7c913d4bc71463d6a7a404e2c6a252a883a4a191e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cf1a12f36de8-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And the first, Your Honor, is the one that I was talking about earlier, that sex and gender identity, like sex and sexual orientation, are different traits. They're defined, they have different definitions, as my friend just said. He agrees that they're different traits. And there's a reason why when Congress wants to prohibit discrimination based on the traits of sexual orientation and gender identity, it lists them separately. It doesn't define sex as including these traits. It's because Congress has recognized there are different traits."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Francisco",
      "case_id": "2019-18-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cf1a12f36de8976e980e1ca7c913d4bc71463d6a7a404e2c6a252a883a4a191e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cf1a12f36de8-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the difference between a transgender man and a cisgender man is that one identifies with his biological sex and the other identifies with the opposite of his biological sex. And that is a very meaningful difference that is not grounded on stereotypes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Francisco",
      "case_id": "2019-18-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cf1a12f36de8976e980e1ca7c913d4bc71463d6a7a404e2c6a252a883a4a191e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cf1a12f36de8-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The place that stereotypes come up are when you're figuring out whether similarly situated -- whether two people are, in fact, similarly situated. An aggressive man -- take Price Waterhouse: An aggressive man is similarly situated to an aggressive woman. They have the exact same trait, aggressiveness, and the only difference is that stereotypical view that women shouldn't be aggressive. But a transgender man and a cisgender man do not ever share the same trait in the first place because one identifies with his biological sex, the other identifies with the opposite of his biological sex."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2019-18-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cf1a12f36de8976e980e1ca7c913d4bc71463d6a7a404e2c6a252a883a4a191e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cf1a12f36de8-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That that would be okay?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2019-18-107",
